WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.215
Effective threat hunting depends on meaningful telemetry.

00:00:03.215 --> 00:00:08.554
Analysts need data that reflects activity across important systems and enough context

00:00:08.554 --> 00:00:13.004
to distinguish expected operations from behavior that deserves further review.

00:00:13.004 --> 00:00:17.453
Endpoint telemetry can describe system activity, software execution, security events,

00:00:17.453 --> 00:00:19.678
connections, and changes over time.

00:00:19.678 --> 00:00:24.573
Its value increases when records include stable asset identifiers, accurate timestamps,

00:00:24.573 --> 00:00:26.353
ownership, and business context.

00:00:26.353 --> 00:00:30.357
Authentication and identity records help hunters understand access patterns.

00:00:30.357 --> 00:00:33.472
Sign-in events, privilege changes, account lifecycle information,

00:00:33.472 --> 00:00:40.591
and identity-provider context can show whether activity aligns with a user's role and normal operating conditions.

00:00:40.591 --> 00:00:42.816
Network telemetry provides another perspective.

00:00:42.816 --> 00:00:45.931
Connection metadata, name-resolution records, approved security-device logs,

00:00:45.931 --> 00:00:53.050
and traffic summaries can reveal relationships among systems without relying on a single endpoint data source.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:56.165
Cloud and application logs are increasingly important.

00:00:56.165 --> 00:00:59.280
Control-plane events, workload records, application audit trails,

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:04.619
and service activity can help explain what occurred in environments that traditional

00:01:04.619 --> 00:01:07.289
network monitoring may not fully represent.

00:01:07.289 --> 00:01:10.403
Asset inventories and configuration information supply context.

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:17.523
A record becomes more useful when the hunter knows whether the system is production or development,

00:01:17.523 --> 00:01:23.307
internet facing or internal, business critical or temporary, and managed by which team.

00:01:23.307 --> 00:01:25.532
Data quality determines investigative quality.

00:01:25.532 --> 00:01:29.537
Missing time periods, inconsistent clocks, duplicate records, short retention,

00:01:29.537 --> 00:01:34.431
unclear field definitions, and incomplete coverage can lead to incorrect conclusions.

00:01:34.431 --> 00:01:37.101
Hunts should document these limitations explicitly.

00:01:37.101 --> 00:01:39.326
Telemetry access also requires governance.

00:01:39.326 --> 00:01:41.996
Organizations should apply authorization, privacy, retention,

00:01:41.996 --> 00:01:47.780
and need-to-know requirements so investigations use security data responsibly and maintain appropriate oversight.
